WEBVTT

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.433
[engine starts]

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.767
[Jerry] <i>This is a 1962 VW split-window</i>
<i>double-cab bus</i>

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:23.023
<i>in dove blue, primer gray, and rust.</i>

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:28.767
<i>The interior is gray vinyl and duct tape.</i>

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.000
<i>It's equipped with a 1971, 60-horsepower,</i>

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.267
<i>dual-port, four-cylinder engine.</i>

00:00:35.836 --> 00:00:38.906
<i>What's cool about this bus, to me,</i>
<i>is that it was used as a service truck</i>

00:00:39.106 --> 00:00:42.543
<i>for a VW Porsche repair shop</i>
<i>in Campbell, California.</i>

00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:46.467
<i>It has a flatbed, two rows of seats</i>

00:00:46.747 --> 00:00:48.549
<i>and an extra door on one side.</i>

00:00:50.751 --> 00:00:52.152
[phone ringing]

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:53.554
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Michael?</i>
<i></i>-[Michael] <i>Jerry!</i>

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.523
-[Jerry] <i>Would you like to get a coffee?</i>
<i></i>-[Michael] <i>Definitely! Come over.</i>

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.092
-[Jerry] <i>I'll be there in a few minutes.</i>
<i></i>-[Michael] <i>Okay.</i>

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.033
[Jerry] <i>Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this</i>
<i>is </i>Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.533
<i>Today, my guest is Michael Richards.</i>

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.700
<i>He played my next-door neighbor</i>
<i>for nine years on TV.</i>

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.443
<i>And we're still very close.</i>

00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:28.989
Why would you drink coffee?
What do you need, you need a boost?

00:01:29.056 --> 00:01:31.692
-You looking for a boost?
-I need attention and I need a boost.

00:01:31.892 --> 00:01:34.127
I want to get your reaction, unvarnished,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.333
to this vehicle that I've chosen
specifically for you.

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:40.634
-Oh, gosh, that's...
-[laughs]

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:41.735
-I love it. I...
-What do you want?

00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:43.837
-This is you, Michael.
-No, I like it. This is me.

00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:46.567
I like this. I can throw my gear in it.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.433
You don't have to have coffee.
It's just the name of the show.

00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:56.133
-I don't think you drink coffee.
-I do.

00:01:56.216 --> 00:01:58.251
What is it? Coffee, liquor, money?

00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:59.920
-Is that your life now?
-[laughing] Yeah.

00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:02.122
-Oh, Jerry!
-Coffee, liquor, money.

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:04.992
-Those are my priorities.
-Coffee, liquor, and a lot of money.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.664
[Michael]
What else can I point out to you?

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.967
Now, this is the Getty Museum
over here on the left.

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:19.167
It was up for sale, Jerry.
They wanted $7.5 million.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.400
Take a look at the front of it.
It's sliding down the hill.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.300
They put that retaining wall
at the bottom

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.700
because they anticipate
more of it to come down.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.549
-[Jerry] Oh, my God.
-And the swimming pool's already gone.

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:31.618
Half the pool's already slid down.

00:02:31.685 --> 00:02:33.720
-[Jerry] Oh, my God.
-Now, look. Look!

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.591
-There's the swimming pool, Jerry. Look!
-[Jerry laughing]

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:41.433
Why would you buy 7.5...
And you know, inevitably,

00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:45.367
it's all coming down.
Just a slight tilt in the axis,

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:48.200
and it's bubbly time.
You're under the sea.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:54.633
[Jerry] Remember we played each other?

00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:57.033
I played you and you played me
in that one episode

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.667
because we switched apartments?
The Kenny Rogers Roasters.

00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:01.867
-God, I'm awful at this.
-The orange sign.

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:03.183
When you opened the door,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.986
that orange light hit you
and you leaned backwards.

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:11.391
-That was one of the funniest bits.
-Oh, my...

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:16.000
But that was the intellectual aspect
of your comedy that I always appreciated.

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:19.133
That the light is so strong,
it's gonna bend my spine.

00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:22.402
-Oh, God. Those were good days.
-Those were good days.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.438
You gave me the role of my lifetime.

00:03:24.533 --> 00:03:27.567
You gave me the experience of my lifetime.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.200
Getting to play with you.

00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:34.367
You know, being around you, Jerry,
I'm gonna turn into that crazy character.

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:36.300
It's gonna happen.
It's gonna gradually happen.

00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:37.333
Go ahead.

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:39.219
[Michael] Can you turn
down one of these streets?

00:03:39.286 --> 00:03:40.120
[Jerry] Yes, which?

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.533
-[Michael] El Medio?
-Yes.

00:03:41.855 --> 00:03:44.391
[Michael] Up here
is where Sugar Ray Leonard lives.

00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:46.133
He lives in this house right here.

00:03:46.193 --> 00:03:47.394
-[Jerry] Which?
-Let's say hello.

00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:49.667
-Are you friends with him?
-Yeah, I know him.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:50.933
He may invite us in.

00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:53.400
[Michael] Sugar!

00:03:54.034 --> 00:03:55.469
Su-- Sugar!

00:03:57.267 --> 00:03:59.600
[Jerry] Hey, Barry.
What are you doing here?

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.175
He told me Sugar Ray Leonard lives here.

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:03.567
<i>This is Barry Katz.</i>

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.567
<i>The last time I saw him was</i>
<i>in my 2002 documentary,</i> Comedian.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.867
All he has to do is just...

00:04:13.153 --> 00:04:15.322
-Are you on your way here?
-[Barry] Yes.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.100
-[Jerry] Really?
-[Michael] Hey, hi.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.200
Is Sugar in?

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.767
-[woman] Sugar in?
-[Michael] Sugar Ray Leonard live here?

00:04:20.867 --> 00:04:21.733
[woman] No.

00:04:21.795 --> 00:04:23.964
[Jerry] Oh, jeez.
Have you got the wrong house?

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:26.300
-[Michael] Whose house is it?
-[Barry] Jay Mohr.

00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:28.833
-Who's Jay Mohr?
-He's a comedian and an actor.

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:31.100
It's the next house. I made a mistake.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.200
-Are you sure?
-I'm sure of it. It's the next house.

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:35.000
So Jay Mohr lives
next to Sugar Ray Leonard?

00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:37.000
We should say hello.
It'd be rude to not say hello.

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:40.167
-[Michael] Is that a real dog? Look.
-[Jerry] I didn't know he was gay.

00:04:40.233 --> 00:04:41.900
-[dog barking]
-[Michael] I'm frightened.

00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:43.867
I'm sure that was his house.

00:04:44.067 --> 00:04:45.167
It's odd that...

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:46.920
Hmm...

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.900
-So, what do you think of it?
-Oh, I think the car's great.

00:04:50.991 --> 00:04:54.027
It makes me want to take things
like this, you know, and just kind of...

00:04:54.094 --> 00:04:56.129
You know... [grunts]

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.800
[speaking gibberish]

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:03.600
Can you imagine how upset
Jay Mohr is gonna be?

00:05:03.867 --> 00:05:05.600
-Why? Really?
-If he finds out.

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:08.633
-[Michael] Did we excuse ourselves well?
-No.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.067
-[Michael] Did we leave properly?
-It was rude.

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:11.533
We should go back.

00:05:11.667 --> 00:05:13.967
No, I'm not going back.
We don't like to be rude but...

00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:17.533
sitcom superstars
that have visited your home,

00:05:17.633 --> 00:05:20.300
how long will you make them wait
out there on the sidewalk?

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.567
He may have been upstairs,
trying to get dressed.

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:24.433
As I said, they were working next door.

00:05:24.533 --> 00:05:25.800
He probably didn't get much sleep.

00:05:25.867 --> 00:05:27.800
Well, come down in a velveteen robe.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.533
If he's got a dog that small,

00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:33.367
-He could have a robe like that.
-He's got a robe, yeah.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:37.267
[Michael] This is a long drive
for a cup of coffee.

00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:39.800
Somebody waylaid me
at Sugar Ray Leonard's.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.933
I'm sorry. I'm awful sorry.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.667
-Now, tell me the truth--
-You know what's gonna happen?

00:05:44.733 --> 00:05:47.000
-Do you really know Sugar Ray?
-Do I really know him?

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.433
-Tell me the truth.
-No.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.533
I don't know him.

00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:57.090
-See, I don't remember the past, too.
-Of course you don't remember the past.

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.000
There's nothing else to remember.

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:04.800
There goes 125 years of psychology
right out the window.

00:06:05.167 --> 00:06:07.467
That kind of behavior, in the past,

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:09.369
-will wind you up in jail.
-Right.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:15.033
Jail is such a... really a way
that a grownup would punish a child.

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.867
You go in there and you stay in there.

00:06:17.967 --> 00:06:21.267
Robbing a 7-Eleven. Get in that cell!

00:06:21.433 --> 00:06:24.967
I mean, to drive a car
through a crowd of people,

00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:28.567
with a sandwich in your hand,
not even looking where you are going.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:31.233
You sit in that cell
and think about what you've done.

00:06:31.333 --> 00:06:34.600
Charles Manson,
that was supposed to be a party.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.600
You should be ashamed of yourself.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.565
And there'll be no dinner tonight.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.667
[Jerry] How weird an experience
was that for Jay Mohr?

00:06:44.933 --> 00:06:48.267
Who was at my front door?
What were they doing?

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:51.167
They were looking
for Sugar Ray Leonard's house.

00:06:51.333 --> 00:06:54.533
-And they called my dog what?
-I don't want to say.

00:06:58.118 --> 00:07:01.888
[Michael] Are we really going in there?
Are you serious? All these people?

00:07:02.033 --> 00:07:05.133
We can't just walk in there going, "Hi."
We're not normal people.

00:07:05.233 --> 00:07:06.933
We're not normal people.

00:07:07.133 --> 00:07:09.067
You just walk right in and go, "I'm here"?

00:07:09.167 --> 00:07:10.733
"I'm here, you deal with it."

00:07:10.864 --> 00:07:12.699
[Michael]
That's why I bring my kit with me.

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:18.000
Now you don't know. See?

00:07:19.433 --> 00:07:21.367
You don't know who I am, do you?

00:07:21.467 --> 00:07:23.433
No. I know you're crazy.

00:07:27.967 --> 00:07:29.300
I don't know, Jerry.

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:31.667
[Michael] Hey, look. There's me.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.933
-[Jerry] That's the same hair.
-[Michael] That's the same guy.

00:07:35.033 --> 00:07:36.900
-Isn't that amazing?
-[Jerry] That's amazing.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.700
[Michael] That's amazing. How you doing?

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.333
[Jerry] I can't believe
it's the same exact hairstyle.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.900
-[Michael] Look.
-[Jerry] Look at that.

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:45.899
-The same sunglasses. Wait a minute.
-You never know who you meet.

00:07:45.966 --> 00:07:47.567
This is unbelievable, isn't it?

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.167
-Yes.
-I could probably hide out as you.

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:52.867
-And I could hide out as you.
-This is unbelievable.

00:07:52.967 --> 00:07:54.133
Life is a hideout.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.167
No one is gonna believe
that that just happened.

00:07:57.633 --> 00:08:00.133
I think you should wear a hat
and some sunglasses or something.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.548
Oh, Michael. Free yourself.

00:08:01.881 --> 00:08:04.184
We're just raindrops
on a windshield, Michael.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.419
[Michael] I wanna know
who's wiping me off.

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:08.488
[inaudible]

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.467
[Jerry laughing]

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:21.567
[Michael] Jesus. Who put that there?

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.200
I was thinking this morning,
I swear to God,

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.033
we would be rehearsing,
something dumb would happen, or weird

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.867
and you would just go,
"Well, that was interesting."

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.100
And the wheels would start to turn.

00:08:54.167 --> 00:08:57.433
That's how you play chess. He makes a move
and you go, "Hmm, that's interesting.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.367
[Jerry laughing]

00:08:58.467 --> 00:08:59.467
Interesting."

00:08:59.567 --> 00:09:02.833
I used to play chess. When I was
in the Army, I played. I was unbeatable.

00:09:02.933 --> 00:09:06.067
I was very, very good at it.
With chess, there's ratings.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.700
And chess master is about 2,100.

00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:11.733
And I was playing a computer
on a 2,100 level.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:12.967
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:15.789
So I had been playing that machine
for weeks,

00:09:16.167 --> 00:09:18.367
and then I happened to be out
on Hollywood Boulevard

00:09:18.433 --> 00:09:22.000
standing on the corner and I saw this man,
tattered, dirty.

00:09:22.067 --> 00:09:23.367
He was a street person.

00:09:23.467 --> 00:09:25.800
-And he had a chess set there to play.
-Right.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.300
And I said, "You play chess?"
He says, "I do, I do."

00:09:29.133 --> 00:09:31.200
-But it's a homeless guy.
-It's a homeless guy.

00:09:31.267 --> 00:09:33.567
I said, "I'll tell you what,
I'll play you a game."

00:09:33.667 --> 00:09:37.233
He goes,
"[mumbles] I'll play you two games.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.433
I beat you two times,
you can't play me no more."

00:09:39.833 --> 00:09:42.567
Puts out his hands, you know,
to see who's gonna go first.

00:09:42.733 --> 00:09:46.500
Black, white. I pick. I'm white.
That means I have the first move.

00:09:46.553 --> 00:09:47.854
I already have the advantage.

00:09:48.167 --> 00:09:50.667
-Sitting on the sidewalk?
-I'm sitting on the sidewalk with him.

00:09:50.733 --> 00:09:52.500
-I'm down here like this.
-Right.

00:09:52.567 --> 00:09:55.267
So I move my piece out,
he moves his piece out very quickly.

00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:57.297
Boom! I said, "Oh, he stops that move."

00:09:57.467 --> 00:09:59.400
So I move a knight out,
he moves out the pawn.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.533
He moves out the bishop...

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:02.567
In two minutes, he's moving--
He's got me in a defense...

00:10:02.635 --> 00:10:04.471
Boom, checkmate! I'm like, "Whoa!"

00:10:04.633 --> 00:10:06.667
He checkmated me in two minutes.

00:10:07.533 --> 00:10:10.667
Nobody has ever checkmated me
in two minutes.

00:10:10.767 --> 00:10:14.733
Nobody. Not even the machine
can checkmate me in two minutes.

00:10:15.067 --> 00:10:18.567
But this time, I said,
"Okay, let's play. Let's play chess."

00:10:18.667 --> 00:10:21.367
He makes me pick. I go first again.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.100
Okay.

00:10:23.523 --> 00:10:27.127
I lean in. He moves out his knight,
I move my bishop...

00:10:27.193 --> 00:10:29.028
-Ba-ba-ba... Checkmate!
-[laughing]

00:10:29.433 --> 00:10:31.100
Faster than the first time.

00:10:32.033 --> 00:10:35.267
So, now he's putting his stuff away.
I'm going, "Come on. Let's play again."

00:10:35.367 --> 00:10:38.700
He goes, "No, no, no. I beat you
two times, you can't play me no more."

00:10:39.067 --> 00:10:42.167
I'm walking down...
Following the guy down the street...

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:43.733
going, "Let's play."

00:10:43.833 --> 00:10:46.600
He goes, "No, no, you leave me alone.
I don't wanna play..."

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.767
I'm going, "Come on,
let's just play another game. Come on."

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:51.333
He wouldn't play me.

00:10:51.633 --> 00:10:54.700
I went home, I called a friend
who's a professional chess player.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:01.333
I called him up. I said, "Leon, I played
a guy on the street who beat me twice."

00:11:01.567 --> 00:11:03.200
He goes, "Yeah, you played a savant.

00:11:03.333 --> 00:11:06.800
When I'm in tournaments in a city, I look
for those guys, to play those guys."

00:11:06.866 --> 00:11:08.835
I say, "You beat them?" He goes, "Never."

00:11:09.833 --> 00:11:11.533
-Really?
-Really.

00:11:11.867 --> 00:11:14.567
I always thought, "Can you get
one of those guys in a tournament?"

00:11:14.633 --> 00:11:17.333
Imagine. He says, "You can't hold them
in place. They're crazy."

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:18.833
But they're unbeatable.

00:11:20.067 --> 00:11:23.067
So he could really be
the greatest chess player in the world.

00:11:23.167 --> 00:11:24.567
Possibly... Most likely.

00:11:24.633 --> 00:11:26.100
He told me in the beginning.

00:11:26.819 --> 00:11:29.222
"I beat you two times, that's it.

00:11:29.289 --> 00:11:31.324
We don't play anymore.
You can't play me anymore."

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.367
Why would he set a rule like that?

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.467
Because he's done it over and over again
and he doesn't have a lot of time

00:11:37.767 --> 00:11:41.800
to be fooling around
with somebody he can beat so easily.

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:43.200
That's probably why.

00:11:43.467 --> 00:11:49.500
What was it that he was able to do
so well that he could win so fast?

00:11:49.667 --> 00:11:55.800
He saw the moves before they took place.
This is where it gets a bit metaphysical.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.900
Perhaps you have to be ultimately crazy.

00:11:58.967 --> 00:12:01.067
-You think he was a clairvoyant savant?
-Or dislodged

00:12:01.167 --> 00:12:05.300
from the kind of reality
that we're all adjusting to.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:08.667
There are other kinds of realities,
other kinds of zones

00:12:08.767 --> 00:12:09.933
-to inhabit.
-Right.

00:12:10.033 --> 00:12:14.000
Great artists have inhabited zones
and then it becomes a new paradigm.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.500
People go, "Wow, we hadn't thought
about going there."

00:12:16.767 --> 00:12:21.633
I invented a chessboard.
I wanted to play it in the round.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.900
So you're, like,
on the north and the south pole,

00:12:23.967 --> 00:12:27.300
and your pieces...
So you can play in all directions.

00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:32.252
That's when I had to stop playing chess.

00:12:35.955 --> 00:12:37.857
-Bernie Brillstein.
-Bernie Brillstein.

00:12:37.933 --> 00:12:40.067
-Showbiz legend, indeed.
-Right.

00:12:40.167 --> 00:12:43.333
That was when I was doing <i>Fridays.</i>
He was executive producer.

00:12:43.433 --> 00:12:44.933
He came in. He's a big man.

00:12:45.798 --> 00:12:49.168
And not just physically,
but his presence. What everybody knew,

00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:51.504
-He was like a lion.
-that when Bernie walked into a room,

00:12:51.571 --> 00:12:53.539
-Right.
-somebody just walked in the room.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:57.067
Says, "What do you think, Bernie?"
He goes, "It's gold."

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:00.833
[Jerry laughing]

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.300
Which then became Banya's line,
on the show.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.700
-You remember that?
-Oh, my God, you gave him that line?

00:13:09.767 --> 00:13:12.067
-"It's gold, Jerry?" Oh, man.
-Yeah.

00:13:12.133 --> 00:13:13.733
You don't miss a beat.
You pick it all up.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.733
Well, it's in the air. It's in the color.

00:13:20.667 --> 00:13:23.500
I remember when we were putting
the show together,

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:27.167
first thing I said when they said,
"Well, how about Michael Richards?"

00:13:27.267 --> 00:13:29.900
I went, "Is he available?
Is Michael Richards available?"

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.933
You had to audition
with two other guys for the network.

00:13:33.033 --> 00:13:35.067
Two others?
You brought me back three times.

00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:37.367
There was a room of different people
to read against.

00:13:37.633 --> 00:13:39.900
But it was like chess.
I knew I was gonna get the part.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.533
-You did?
-I knew it on the first time I met you.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.533
-Really?
-I knew it. I never said a thing.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:45.933
And I never said it to you.

00:13:46.433 --> 00:13:51.033
I knew when I started reading with you.
I just said, "They can't pass that up."

00:13:51.467 --> 00:13:55.067
You just feel the hand of the universe
going, "You two are gonna be together."

00:13:55.267 --> 00:13:57.967
But I could've played Kramer
for the rest of my life.

00:13:58.033 --> 00:13:58.867
Yes.

00:13:58.933 --> 00:14:01.367
That character would fit
into any situation.

00:14:01.733 --> 00:14:05.733
There was a great universality
to the soul of that character.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.667
-I went to Bali. Get away.
-Right.

00:14:09.182 --> 00:14:12.719
Get away for a little while.
Like to just step away from the world.

00:14:13.267 --> 00:14:16.800
I go out into the jungle.
I'm way deep into the jungle now.

00:14:16.867 --> 00:14:19.433
And I see some naked people,
and they point at me. They go...

00:14:19.567 --> 00:14:21.133
"[speaking gibberish] Kramer!"

00:14:22.367 --> 00:14:23.400
"This is Kramer."

00:14:24.267 --> 00:14:25.633
They recognized me from the show.

00:14:25.733 --> 00:14:29.767
They had a hut with a cable
out there somewhere. They could watch--

00:14:29.833 --> 00:14:32.733
They had a little television.
They watched the show.

00:14:33.033 --> 00:14:34.567
In the jungles of Bali.

00:14:36.067 --> 00:14:37.933
You know those performers
who just love it?

00:14:38.033 --> 00:14:41.267
-Yeah.
-It was always a struggle with me.

00:14:41.314 --> 00:14:42.148
[groans]

00:14:42.433 --> 00:14:45.900
No, no.
I don't accept the judging of process.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:47.333
Doesn't matter that you rehearse

00:14:47.433 --> 00:14:50.200
with your nose up against the flat,
saying lines.

00:14:50.267 --> 00:14:51.867
-Doesn't matter.
-You used to see doing that?

00:14:51.933 --> 00:14:54.733
Yes. We're all trying to get
to the same island.

00:14:55.667 --> 00:15:00.833
Whether you swim, fly, surf,
or skydive in, it doesn't matter.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.735
What matters
is when the red light comes on.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.000
Okay. Because sometimes
I look back at the show

00:15:06.067 --> 00:15:08.033
and I think I should have
enjoyed myself more.

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:10.567
Michael, I could say that myself.

00:15:10.967 --> 00:15:12.400
But that was not our job.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:16.333
Our job is not for us to enjoy.
Our job is to make sure they enjoy it.

00:15:16.416 --> 00:15:17.950
-And that's what we did.
-Ah.

00:15:18.033 --> 00:15:20.300
You know, that's beautiful.
That's beautiful.

00:15:20.367 --> 00:15:22.367
Because I think I worked selfishly.

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:24.067
And not selflessly.

00:15:24.167 --> 00:15:26.533
-It's not about me. It's about them.
-Yes. No.

00:15:26.633 --> 00:15:27.633
Yes.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:31.400
Now, that's a lesson I learned
seven years ago,

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:33.300
when I blew it in the comedy club.

00:15:33.367 --> 00:15:35.467
Lost my temper
because somebody interrupted my act

00:15:35.535 --> 00:15:37.403
and said some things that hurt me,

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:39.539
-and I lashed out in anger.
-Right. Right.

00:15:39.733 --> 00:15:42.700
I should have been working
selflessly that evening.

00:15:42.833 --> 00:15:45.800
Most of the time,
when I'm in that zone, I am selfless.

00:15:45.867 --> 00:15:48.600
Right. You told me that you had done
a couple of sets.

00:15:49.133 --> 00:15:50.900
-No, I never--
-Do you want to?

00:15:51.033 --> 00:15:54.600
Sometimes, I say, "I must. I should."
And normally, I would have...

00:15:55.154 --> 00:15:56.155
[mutters]

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:00.067
...gone in and played around
with this material.

00:16:00.159 --> 00:16:05.064
But, no. No, I... [stammers]

00:16:05.133 --> 00:16:08.700
I busted up after that event
seven years ago. It broke me down.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.167
It was a selfish response.

00:16:12.533 --> 00:16:15.833
I took it too personally.
And I should have just said,

00:16:16.008 --> 00:16:18.010
"Yeah, you're absolutely right.
I'm not funny.

00:16:18.077 --> 00:16:20.913
I'll go home and work on my material.
I'll see you tomorrow night."

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.382
[speaks gibberish]
And split. Or something. Anything.

00:16:23.733 --> 00:16:26.500
But, you know,
it's just one of those nights.

00:16:27.386 --> 00:16:29.021
And thanks for sticking by me.

00:16:29.733 --> 00:16:31.567
-Really, you...
-There was no issue with that.

00:16:31.633 --> 00:16:34.667
-Well, I mean, it meant a lot to me.
-That's nice.

00:16:35.133 --> 00:16:38.200
But inside,
it still kicks me around a bit.

00:16:38.267 --> 00:16:41.100
Okay, well, that's... You know...
That's up to you.

00:16:41.167 --> 00:16:44.500
-Yeah, [stammers] that's a big...
-That's up to you

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:47.507
to say, "You know,
I've been carrying this bag long enough.

00:16:47.573 --> 00:16:48.574
-Yeah.
-I'm gonna put it down."

00:16:49.367 --> 00:16:51.267
Yeah. Yeah...

00:16:55.982 --> 00:16:57.984
[inaudible]

00:17:58.367 --> 00:18:01.600
[Jerry] Well, I hope that you do consider
using your instrument again...

00:18:01.667 --> 00:18:02.633
[Michael] Thanks.

00:18:02.682 --> 00:18:04.450
[Jerry] ...because it's the most
beautiful instrument I've seen.

00:18:04.533 --> 00:18:07.400
-[Michael] Oh, Jerry. Thanks, buddy.
-It is. Yeah.